# ssh client configuration.
#
# ssh takes the FIRST value it finds for each keyword, so this Include has to
# stay at the top: anything dropped into config.d then takes precedence over the
# defaults below. IdentityFile is the exception — multiple entries accumulate
# rather than override, so drop-ins add keys to the list instead of replacing
# it, which is what a second identity wants.
#
# config.d is not managed by chezmoi. A missing or empty directory is fine: a
# glob matching nothing is not an error.
Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*

Host github.com
  User git
  # Key naming scheme: id_<account>_<service>. The old name here was
  # `github_rsa`, which was both inaccurate (the key is ed25519) and ambiguous
  # once a machine holds more than one GitHub key — nothing in the name said
  # which account it authenticates as.
  #
  # Per-user by construction — each user has their own key at this path inside
  # their own home directory, so no templating is needed.
  #
  # This block was previously appended to ~/.ssh/config by zsh/tools/github.zsh
  # on every single shell start, guarded only by a grep.
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_brisberg_github

  # Offer ONLY the key named above. Without this, ssh also offers every key the
  # agent holds — and GitHub decides which account you are from the first key it
  # accepts, not from which key you meant. So on any machine whose agent has
  # picked up a second GitHub key, the default identity silently becomes
  # whichever one the agent happens to list first, including for pushes to this
  # repository. Observed in practice, not theorised.
  #
  # A stale key is enough on its own: `ssh-add` keeps a key in memory after the
  # file is deleted, so removing a key file does not retire it. `ssh-add -D`
  # does.
  #
  # This costs nothing on a machine with one key, which is why it belongs here
  # rather than in a drop-in: it makes the default deterministic everywhere.
  IdentitiesOnly yes
